            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Tissues in Action"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.857,
            0.018,
            0.952,
            0.088
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "N 29"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.088,
            0.775,
            0.141
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "in animals, muscle tissue enables movement and nervous tissue carries messages to different parts of the body. In plants, conducting tissues, such as xylem transports water and minerals, while phloem transports food."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.109,
            0.15,
            0.734,
            0.171
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3.1 Why are Plant and Animal Tissues Different?"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.174,
            0.686,
            0.327
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In Chapter 2, Cell: The Building Block of Life, you compared plant and animal cells, and studied the major differences between them. Most plants are fixed in one place and do not move from place to place like animals. They need support to stay firm and upright. Plant cells have a cell wall that provides rigidity and strength. In general, animals can move (although some, such as sponges, are immobile). Without a rigid cell wall, animal cells can change shape easily. This cellular flexibility eventually helps make their bodies suitable for locomotion."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.329,
            0.687,
            0.5
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Another major difference between plants and animals is their mode of nutrition. Animals have tissues that help them digest food obtained from different food sources, while plants have tissues that help them utilise solar energy for synthesising the food components through photosynthesis. Plants and animals have distinct tissues for transporting food and water to different parts of the body. The growth patterns in plants and animals also vary because the tissues responsible for growth differ in structure and function. In this chapter, we will learn how the structures of plant and animal tissues relate to their specific functions."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.511,
            0.52,
            0.532
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3.2 Tissues for Growth in Plants"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.536,
            0.685,
            0.605
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "You must have observed that a small seedling grows into a tall tree, roots grow deep into the soil, stems become thicker with time and grass grows again after being eaten by grazing animals. Which tissues are responsible for these changes?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.139,
            0.607,
            0.43,
            0.625
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Plants grow in different ways —"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.118,
            0.627,
            0.622,
            0.645
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "y increase in "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "length",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " (height of stem and depth of roots),"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.118,
            0.647,
            0.511,
            0.665
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "y increase in "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "girth",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " (thickness of stem), and"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.117,
            0.668,
            0.669,
            0.686
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "y "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "regrowth",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " after cutting the branches or grazing by animals."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.109,
            0.688,
            0.773,
            0.722
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "This growth require actively dividing cells that together form a tissue called a meristematic tissue."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.138,
            0.722,
            0.621,
            0.74
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Let us explore different kinds of meristematic tissues."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.749,
            0.715,
            0.79
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3.2.1. Apical meristem — How do plants grow in length? Let us study the growth of roots in an onion bulb."
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.798,
            0.512,
            0.817
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Activity 3.1: Let us design experiments"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.121,
            0.819,
            0.681,
            0.838
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "1. Take two glass jars or couplin jars and fill them with water."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.121,
            0.84,
            0.772,
            0.859
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2. Now, take two onion bulbs and place one in each jar, as shown in Fig. 3.1."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 18,
          "bbox": [
            0.121,
            0.861,
            0.659,
            0.879
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3. Observe the growth of roots in both bulbs for a few days."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 19,
          "bbox": [
            0.121,
            0.882,
            0.567,
            0.899
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "4. Measure the length of roots on days 1, 2 and 3."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 20,
          "bbox": [
            0.732,
            0.178,
            0.899,
            0.195
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Meet a Scientist"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
